  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none; full paper text, including appendices, is present.",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper evaluates textual unlearning as a cross-modal safety defense, but does not test the specified fine-tuning, first-token, localized-parameter, or deepening claims. It cites related safety and unlearning work without relying on the claim being cataloged.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports attack success rates after textual unlearning, multimodal unlearning, and SFT. The retained rows record textual-unlearning results and human-evaluation results as related safety findings; they do not measure any listed claim version. The paper does not test compliant-opening prefills, adversarial suffixes as a mechanism for bypassing the opening, decoding changes, per-token KL, or safety localization in parameters.",
    "test_reason": "The paper tests text-only unlearning as a defense for VLMs, measured by attack success rate on cross-modal jailbreak benchmarks. Its results do not bear on any version. It does no harmful or benign fine-tuning that raises compliance (V1, V2), no per-token KL (V3), no prefill, suffix or decoding attacks (V4) and no localization analysis (V5). Section 5 does discuss a shallow-alignment-like mechanism: once SFT models emit the 'first few harmful tokens', context contamination continues the harm, while unlearned models 'dynamically' steer away. That is supported only by single qualitative examples (Table 4), with no rate measured. So it bears on V6/V7 as discussion, not as a test. 'discusses' is a better relation than 'cites_only'.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The authors conclude that text-only unlearning on the LLM component greatly reduces ASR for text and vision-text attacks while preserving utility. They find multimodal unlearning and SFT less effective and about six times more costly. They attribute this to harm coverage of the dataset mattering more than multimodality.",
    "recipe": "none",
    "recipe_differences": "The paper studies textual and multimodal unlearning and multimodal SFT for VLM safety; it does not reproduce the original claim’s fine-tuning, token-position KL, or opening-bypass methods.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The reported ASR varies by model, prompt dataset, and evaluation method (Llama Guard, target-string matching, or human evaluation). The paper notes that automatic methods can misclassify some harmless refusals.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Text-only unlearning of the LLM component lowered ASR on text and vision-text jailbreak benchmarks across four VLMs, by Llama Guard, target-string and human evaluation. Example: LLaVA-1.5-7B Jailbreak in Pieces fell from 75.69 to 7.52 (Llama Guard).",
        "proposed_claim": "Textual unlearning of a VLM's LLM component reduces harmful-response rates on cross-modal jailbreak benchmarks.",
