    "code_released": "not stated",
    "peer_reviewed": "Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing",
    "problems": "The paper studies only 7B language-parameter models in its main experiments and uses QLoRA; it says larger models and other modalities need further study. The authors note limited coverage in the multimodal harmful datasets and that automatic ASR metrics can misclassify harmless responses. Human evaluation involved three volunteers; agreement is reported, but per-result uncertainty intervals are not stated.",
    "rows_left_out": "Table 1 and Table 7 also report PKU-RLHF Train ASR values for the training-seen dataset. Those eight paired results (four per table across the reported models and judges) are omitted; the retained table rows cover the stated test prompts and attack benchmarks. Other methods in Tables 1 and 6, utility measures in Table 2, and illustrative response examples are not recorded because they are not textual-unlearning ASR outcomes used for the related finding summarized here.",
    "untested": [
      {
        "version": "V1",
        "parts": "No fine-tuning on harmful examples toward compliance; training targets refusals."
      },
      {
        "version": "V2",
        "parts": "No benign fine-tuning and no measurement of harmful compliance after it."
      },
      {
        "version": "V3",
        "parts": "No per-token KL or position-wise analysis between aligned and base models."
      },
      {
        "version": "V4",
        "parts": "No prefilling, suffix or decoding attacks. The cross-modal jailbreaks are not isolated as opening-bypass mechanisms."
      },
      {
        "version": "V5",
        "parts": "LoRA (rank 32, q/v projections) is used only as a training tool; no localization or ablation."
      },
      {
        "version": "V6",
        "parts": "Section 5 hypothesizes that SFT overfits and lets harmful openings continue (context contamination). Untested: no measurement."
      },
      {
        "version": "V7",
        "parts": "The claim that unlearned models recover after harmful first tokens rests on one example (Table 4). There is no rate of recovery after a harmful prefix, and no position-wise robustness test."
      }
    ],
    "missing": [],
    "verdict_notes": "Relation changed from cites_only to discusses, because Section 5 and Table 4 explicitly discuss continuation after the first few harmful tokens. That is qualitative only, so no verdicts are given. The study cites the V1/V2 original source (arXiv:2310.03693) only for SFT overfitting. All rows correctly carry versions ['none']."
  },
  "versions": [
    {
      "version": "V1",
      "status": "not_tested",
      "untested_parts": "Whether a few to a few hundred harmful examples fine-tuned to harmful outputs remove refusal in safety-trained models. The paper's unlearning and SFT methods instead train toward refusals or other safe outputs."
    },
    {
      "version": "V2",
      "status": "not_tested",
      "untested_parts": "Whether ordinary or carefully selected benign fine-tuning data raises harmful compliance."
    },
    {
      "version": "V3",
      "status": "not_tested",
      "untested_parts": "Per-token KL between aligned and base models across response positions."
    },
    {
      "version": "V4",
      "status": "not_tested",
      "untested_parts": "Whether compliant-opening prefills, suffixes, or decoding changes bypass safety by getting past initial response tokens. The paper evaluates jailbreak benchmarks but does not isolate or test those mechanisms."
    },
    {
      "version": "V5",
      "status": "not_tested",
      "untested_parts": "Whether a small set of weights or ranks carries safety behavior. LoRA is used for training, but the paper does not test safety localization or ablate ranks or parameters."
    },
    {
      "version": "V6",
      "status": "not_tested",
      "untested_parts": "Whether data or training objectives cause safety behavior to be shallow."
    },
    {
      "version": "V7",
      "status": "not_tested",
      "untested_parts": "Whether training that spreads safety beyond the first tokens deepens safety, or whether such deepening holds up."
    }
  ],
